/* book Worked example: Q: An interior panel of a flat slab floor is 6.0 m × 6.0 m with 450 mm square columns. Slab 200 mm, drops 2.0 m × 2.0 m with total depth 250 mm. Live load 4 kN/m², finish 1 kN/m², M20. Find the strip moments by the direct design method and check punching shear.
   1. Conditions: three or more spans each way (assumed), square panels, live load 4 < 3 × dead load. Drop 2.0 m ≥ 6.0/3 = 2.0 m.
   2. Loads: slab 0.200 × 25 = 5.00; drops averaged over the panel 0.05 × 25 × 4/36 = 0.139; finish 1.0; live 4.0; w = 10.139 kN/m², wu = 15.208 kN/m².
   3. lₙ = 6.0 − 0.45 = 5.55 m (more than 0.65 × 6.0 = 3.90 m). W = 15.208 × 6.0 × 5.55 = 506.4 kN; M₀ = 506.4 × 5.55/8 = 351.3 kN·m.
   4. Negative 0.65 × 351.3 = 228.4 kN·m; positive 0.35 × 351.3 = 123.0 kN·m.
   5. Strips: column strip 2 × 0.25 × 6.0 = 3.0 m; middle strip 3.0 m.
   6. Per metre: column strip negative 0.75 × 228.4/3.0 = 57.1; middle strip negative 0.25 × 228.4/3.0 = 19.0; column strip positive 0.60 × 123.0/3.0 = 24.6; middle strip positive 0.40 × 123.0/3.0 = 16.4 kN·m/m.
   7. Punching at the column: d at the drop ≈ 250 − 35 = 215 mm; perimeter side 450 + 215 = 665 mm, b₀ = 2660 mm. Vu = 15.208 × (36 − 0.665²) = 540.8 kN; τv = 540774/(2660 × 215) = 0.946 N/mm². ks = 1 (square column), τc = 0.25√20 = 1.118 N/mm². Safe.
   8. Punching outside the drop: d = 200 − 35 = 165 mm; side 2000 + 165 = 2165 mm; Vu = 15.208 × (36 − 2.165²) = 476.2 kN; τv = 0.333 N/mm². Safe.
   9. Without the drop the 200 mm slab gives τv = 1.33 N/mm² at the column, more than 1.118 (but under 1.5 × 1.118 = 1.68): it would need shear reinforcement. That is why the drop is used.
   10. Thickness: 6000/165 = 36.4; with the column strip mid-span steel (pt ≈ 0.26 %) the factor is about 1.56, so the limit is 26 × 1.56 = 40.6. Safe.
   Answer: M₀ = 351.3 kN·m; column strip 57.1 (negative) and 24.6 (positive) kN·m/m; middle strip 19.0 and 16.4 kN·m/m. Punching τv = 0.95 and 0.33 N/mm², both under 1.12 N/mm². */
